Released by Wonder in 1972, &#8220;Superstition&#8221; was his first No. 1 hit on the Billboard Hot 100 in nine years. It went on to win two Grammy Awards, for best rhythm & blues song and best R&B vocal performance, male, and was inducted into the Grammy Hall of Fame in 1998.

Saturday&#8217;s show was the penultimate date of the two acts&#8217; latest road trek together, which wraps up Sunday night (Aug. 16) in Milwaukee. Adams will continue promoting his latest album, Roll With the Punches , with four shows in his native Ontario later this month, while Benatar & Giraldo have August shows in Las Vegas and Rancho Mirage, California, followed by a handful of West Coast concerts, mostly in California, in early fall.

Thanks to its proximity to Windsor, Ontario, just across the Detroit River, Adams also found a particularly receptive audience for his latest single, &#8220;51st State,&#8221; a protest response he described as &#8220;a love letter to Canada,&#8221; offering defiant opposition to U.S. President Donald Trump&#8217;s controversial remarks about annexing the country.

&#8220;I had the song for the documentary and suddenly I had &#8217;51st State&#8217;,&#8221; Adams told Billboard earlier this month. &#8220;I thought it would be perfect to put out on Canada Day (July 1), so we did it.&#8221;

Adams acknowledged that the song is more overtly political than what he typically writes, adding, &#8220;but why not? If certain people can say what they want, so can I. It&#8217;s just his garbage. You want to think people can see through that stupidity, but I don&#8217;t know.&#8221;

The documentary project also inspired Adams to write more songs about his homeland that will be part of his next album, though he&#8217;s not announced any specific details about it yet.
